    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\chi=2$.\n\nRemoving one face decreases $\\chi$ by $1$. Triangulation and suitable boundary-triangle removals preserve it, leaving one triangle with $\\chi=1$.",
      "worked": "Remove one face. This leaves $V$ and $E$ unchanged and decreases $F$ by $1$, so the Euler characteristic decreases by $1$. Because the polyhedron is convex, the remaining surface can be projected into a planar polygonal region without changing these counts. Count only its remaining faces, not the unbounded exterior region.\n\nTriangulate every face by adding diagonals. Each diagonal increases $E$ and $F$ by $1$, so $V-E+F$ is unchanged.\n\nRemove boundary triangles successively, choosing removals that leave a connected triangulated region. If a removed triangle has one boundary edge, delete that edge and the face:\n$$\\Delta\\chi=0-(-1)+(-1)=0.$$\nIf it has two boundary edges, delete those edges, their common boundary vertex and the face:\n$$\\Delta\\chi=(-1)-(-2)+(-1)=0.$$\nContinue until one triangle remains. It has three vertices, three edges and one face, so\n$$\\chi_{\\text{remaining}}=3-3+1=1.$$\nAll steps after removing the original face preserved the characteristic. Restoring that face increases it by $1$, giving\n$$\\chi_{\\text{original}}=1+1=2.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 156
